<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Africa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Africa is the second-largest continent and the home of many of the empires covered by the site, from the Kushite and Aksumite empires to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> empires. Its trade in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, salt and later enslaved people connected it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, Islamic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and Atlantic worlds. From the fifteenth century onwards European powers such as the Portuguese, British, French and Italian empires established colonies, culminating in the partition of the continent in the late nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/africa.jpg" alt="Africa" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin23230 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Africa_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Roman Republic destroyed Carthage at the end of the Third Punic War in 146 BCE, it turned the city's remaining territory in what is now northern Tunisia into the province of <b>Africa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The great stelae of Aksum, among the most ambitious monuments in ancient <b>Africa</b>, required technical skill and organisation, and they shaped later Ethiopian building traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Africa</b> the Vandal kings styled themselves kings of the Vandals and Alans until the Byzantine conquest of 533–534.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Soba was a trading hub on north-south and east-west routes, and goods reached the kingdom from Makuria, the Middle East, western <b>Africa</b>, India and China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Asante system of government was among the most developed constitutional arrangements in pre-colonial <b>Africa</b>, and it combined sacred monarchical authority with institutional checks on royal power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Maryse Condé’s novel Ségou (1984) brought the kingdom to readers worldwide, and Mungo Park’s Travels in the Interior Districts of <b>Africa</b> (1799) gave Europe its first eyewitness picture of the middle Niger and its walled cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
